6" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Trastámara</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Trastámara was a Spanish royal dynasty named after the county of Trastámara in Galicia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trencavel"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-trencavel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Trencavel</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Trencavel were a family of viscounts in Languedoc who in the eleventh and twelfth centuries held Albi, Béziers, Carcassonne, Nîmes and Agde, shifting allegiance…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-tudor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-tudor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Tudor</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Tudor was the royal family of Welsh origin that ruled England and Ireland from 1485 to 1603.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-valois.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Valois</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Valois was a branch of the Capetian dynasty that ruled France from 1328, when Philip VI succeeded the last direct Capetian, until 1589.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois-angouleme"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-valois-angouleme.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Valois-Angoulême</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Valois-Angoulême was a branch of the French royal Valois dynasty, descended from John, Count of Angoulême, a younger son of Louis I, Duke of Orléans.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-varano"><span class="enc-name">House of Varano</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Varano were a noble family who ruled Camerino in the Marche from the second half of the 13th century until 1539.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-vasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-vasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Vasa</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Vasa was a Swedish royal dynasty founded by Gustav I, who became King of Sweden in 1523.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-visconti"><span class="enc-name">House of Visconti</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Visconti was the noble family that ruled Milan from the late thirteenth century until 1447, first as lords and from 1395 as dukes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-waldburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-waldburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Waldburg</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Waldburg is a Swabian noble family named after Waldburg castle near Ravensburg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-welf.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Welf</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Welf, or Guelph, is a European dynasty of Swabian and Bavarian origin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-wettin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Wettin</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Wettin was a German dynasty that ruled Saxony.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-wisdom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Wisdom</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Wisdom, or Bayt al-Hikma, was a major intellectual institution in Baghdad under the Abbasid Caliphate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-wittelsbach.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Wittelsbach</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Wittelsbach is a German dynasty that ruled Bavaria, as duchy, electorate and kingdom, from 1180 to 1918 and the Electoral Palatinate from 1214.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wonders"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-wonders.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Wonders</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Wonders, Beit al-Ajaib, is a ceremonial palace in Stone Town, Zanzibar, built for Sultan Barghash bin Said and completed in 1883.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/house-of-zahringen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">House of Zähringen</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The House of Zähringen was a noble dynasty of south-western Germany, named after Zähringen Castle near Freiburg im Breisgau.</span></a></li></ul>
